@misc{bnd-genre-emit,
  section  = {engine},
  claim    = {the GENERATOR emits a genre check exactly where a project DECLARES one --- bibtex.bzl is the build graph and its only prior claim was three regexes over its TEXT, so this asserts its OUTPUT: a project declaring a genres table gets a :genres target that INVOKES each declared cmd, and a project declaring none gets no target, since a check over an empty registry is green by vacuity},
  check    = {cmd:python3 ../paperkit/tests/boundaries_genre_emit.py},
  % ⚑ Ζ·reads·file — DECLARED PER FILE.  `reads` names a PACKAGE and stages its whole filegroup,
  % which flattens "this witness opens bibtex.bzl" into "this witness depends on tools/", re-keying
  % the cell on every unrelated edit in that package.  Operator's ruling: "I don't like directories
  % as read targets.  That's a flattening of what's actually going on under the hood."  `builds`
  % carries Bazel LABELS and joins `data` directly (bibtex.bzl:270), so the FILE is expressible.
  % MEASURED control: bnd-check and bnd-toplevel read MODULE.bazel, DECLARE it, and pass; these read
  % it, declared nothing, and came back baseline=false in a pristine sandbox.
  % ⚑ Ζ·genre·stage — THE 13 paper.toml FILES ARE INPUTS, AND THEY WERE NOT DECLARED.
  % The witness stats every wired project's paper.toml to partition declaring from quiet;
  % `builds` named only the generator and the manifest, so in a cell NO toml was staged and every
  % project read as "declares NONE" — the host says 1 declaring / 12 not, the cell's record said
  % 13 not.  Enumerated per FILE (the same 13 labels arch-roster-wired carries), never as
  % packages: a package read flattens what is actually being opened.
  builds   = {@@//:MODULE.bazel, @@//tools:bibtex.bzl, @@//:paper.toml, @@//arch:paper.toml, @@//boundaries:paper.toml, @@//config:paper.toml, @@//demo:paper.toml, @@//guide:paper.toml, @@//image:paper.toml, @@//paper:paper.toml, @@//paperkit/library:paper.toml, @@//render:paper.toml, @@//report:paper.toml, @@//setup:paper.toml, @@//talk:paper.toml}
}

